Let $h$ be the height of the liquid when the cone is held with its point up and its base horizontal. Note that the liquid holds a cone whose base is parallel to the base of the whole cone. Also, the liquid holds a smaller cone whose base is at the bottom as well.

[asy]
size(3cm);
pair A,B,C,D;
A=(0,0);
B=(1,0);
C=(0,7);
D=(-1.5,0);
draw(Circle((A+A+B)/2,1));
draw(B--A,D--A,B--C,D--B);
draw((B+A)/2--(C+A)/2);
label("$h$",(B+A)/2,(C+A)/2);
dot(D);
label($"5\sqrt[3]{2}$",A,SE);
[/asy]

Since the bases are parallel, the liquid holds 2 cones similar to the whole cone. Then, from above, the smaller cone is similar to the bigger cone.

The ratios of the smaller cone and the bigger cone are $\frac{h}{12}$ and $\frac{5\sqrt[3]{2}}{12}$. Therefore, $\frac{h}{12} = \frac{5\sqrt[3]{2}}{12}$, from which we obtain $\boxed{17}$ as the value of $m+n+p$.

